MCP Dockerfile

Most lines are identical.

The interesting part is:

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y ...


⸻

Why does MCP need this?

You have:

weasyprint

which generates PDFs.

WeasyPrint is not pure Python.

It depends on Linux libraries.

Example:

libpango
libcairo
gdk-pixbuf


⸻

Why install them?

Without them:

pip install succeeds

but later:

PDF generation crashes


⸻

Significance

Adds operating-system-level dependencies.

Think:

requirements.txt
=
Python dependencies

apt-get install
=
Linux dependencies


⸻

mkdir Command

RUN mkdir -p /MCP/data /MCP/credentials


⸻

What happens?

Creates folders.

/MCP/data
/MCP/credentials


⸻

Why?

Your application expects these folders.

Without them:

FileNotFoundError

may occur.

⸻

Significance

Preparing environment before application starts.

⸻

Docker Ignore

.git
rohan_env
venv
__pycache__
venv1
env2
token.pickle


⸻

What problem does this solve?

When Docker executes:

COPY . .

it copies everything.

Without .dockerignore:

Entire venv
Git History
Cache Files
Temporary Files

would be copied.

⸻

Why is that bad?

Bigger image

Instead of:

500 MB

you get:

2-5 GB


⸻

Slower build

More files
More copying
More upload time


⸻

Security risk

Imagine:

token.pickle

contains credentials.

Now credentials become part of image.

⸻

Significance

.dockerignore is the Docker equivalent of .gitignore.

It prevents unnecessary and sensitive files from entering the image.

⸻

The Most Important Realization

Your Dockerfile is not for Azure.

Many beginners think:

Dockerfile → Azure

No.

Your Dockerfile is creating a portable machine image.

Then:

Azure Container Instance
AWS ECS/Fargate
Local Docker Run
Kubernetes

can all use the exact same image.

That’s why when you move from Azure to AWS, 90% of this Dockerfile remains unchanged. Only the deployment platform changes; the image-building process stays almost identical.
